  {
   "id": "e2-3",
   "name": "The Simple Present tense"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "We use the Simple Present for habits (“I walk to school”), facts (“Water boils at 100 °C”) and timetables (“The bus leaves at seven”).\nWith he, she, it or one person or thing, add -s or -es: he walks, she washes, it carries → carries, have → has.\nNegative: don't / doesn't + the verb without -s: “Tabe doesn't take the bus.”\nQuestions: Do / Does + subject + verb: “Does the bus stop here?” — “Yes, it does.”\nAdverbs of frequency (always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely, never) go before the main verb but after be: “She always takes a taxi.” “The bus is never late.”\nSome verbs are not used in the -ing form when they mean a state: know, like, want, need, believe. Say “I know him”, not “I am knowing him”.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": "Bih",
   "v": "catch",
   "c": "a taxi",
   "f": "usually"
  },
  "problem": "Put the adverb in the right place, then make the sentence negative and a question: {n} {thirdPerson(v)} {c} to school. ({f})",
  "steps": [
   "The adverb goes before the main verb: {n} {f} {thirdPerson(v)} {c} to school.",
   "Negative: doesn't + the verb without -s: {n} doesn't {f} {v} {c} to school.",
   "Question: Does + subject + verb: Does {n} {f} {v} {c} to school?"
  ],
  "answer": "{n} {f} {thirdPerson(v)} {c} to school. / {n} doesn't {f} {v} {c} to school. / Does {n} {f} {v} {c} to school?",
  "checks": [
   "thirdPerson(v) == 'catches'"
  ]
 },
